          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "high",
            "summary": "SherpaMind matches its SherpaDesk purpose, but it needs review because it can send API credentials to a configurable endpoint and writes sensitive support data to local artifacts without strong permission controls.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "The local storage and API access are proportionate to a SherpaDesk backend, but the API base URL is environment/configurable without validation before Basic credentials are sent, and generated documents/exports may include emails, phone numbers, internal notes, and workpad text under a public-named tree without explicit restrictive file modes.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Bootstrap creates a workspace-local venv and installs pinned Python packages from PyPI, then installs the local package in editable mode. This is disclosed and purpose-aligned, but it is a supply-chain and local-code-execution surface without hash verification.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The trigger text includes generic support/ticket/account/technician requests, while the skill can access sensitive local support data, run setup commands, export records, and manage background runtime. High-risk actions are documented as commands, but the skill does not provide a narrow permission boundary or consistent confirmation gate.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The user-level systemd service and foreground service loop are disclosed and treated as optional; setup does not install the service by default. The setup path does remove old SherpaMind-named OpenClaw cron jobs by default, which is scoped and documented but still a host-side mutation users should understand.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The SKILL.md, README, and Python package coherently describe and implement a local SherpaDesk ingest, sync, retrieval, reporting, and OpenClaw query layer. The behavior is not a simple instruction-only skill, and the core capabilities match the stated purpose.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "note"
              }
            ],
